#* {{quote-book|en|year=1590|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=The Faerie Queene|section=III.1|passage=Long were it to describe the goodly frame, / And stately '''port''' of Castle Joyeous {{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=Him I accuse / The city '''ports''' by this hath enter'd|author=[[w:William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]]|title=[[w:Coriolanus (play)|Coriolanus]]|year=1623|section=V.vi}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=And from their ivory '''port''' the Cherubim, / Forth issuing at the accustomed hour|author=[[w:John Milton|Milton]]|title=w:Paradise Lost|year=1667|volume_plain=book IV}}
# An opening or doorway in the side of a [[ship]], especially for boarding or loading; an [[embrasure]] through which a cannon may be discharged; a [[porthole]].
#* {{quote-book|en|passage={{...}} her '''ports''' being within sixteen inches of the water {{...}}|author=[[w:Walter Raleigh|Sir W. Raleigh]]|year=c. 1615|title=A Discourse of the Invention of Ships, Anchors, Compass{{...}}}}
